**Horace Danby** is about fifty. Everyone in his town thinks of him as a good, respectable citizen. He is unmarried, lives quietly with a housekeeper, and makes and repairs **locks** for a living — so he knows more about locks and safes than almost anyone.

But Horace has a secret. Once a year he robs a safe. He does it to pay for his one passion: **rare and expensive books**, which he loves to collect. He plans each robbery so carefully that in fifteen years he has never been caught. After each job he deposits the money in a safe deposit and lives on it for the year, buying books through an agent.

This year his target is **Shotover Grange**, a large country house. Horace studies the house for **two weeks**. He learns:
- the family is away in London;
- the servants go to the cinema on a particular afternoon;
- there is a dog called **Sammy**, who sleeps in a basket and might bark;
- the safe is hidden behind a painting in the drawing room;
- there is a key to the back door kept above it.

He has all his tools ready and plans to wear **gloves** so he leaves no fingerprints. Only one thing troubles him: he suffers from **hay fever**, and the garden of Shotover Grange is full of flowers that make him sneeze.

**Notice the contradiction** in Horace: he is honest in his public life and kind in manner, yet a thief by choice. He justifies his crime because it is 'only once a year' and harms no one physically. The story will show that this self-image is fragile.

Around 2,500 years ago, followers of Pythagoras in Greece believed that every length could be written as a ratio of two whole numbers. Then someone drew a square of side 1 and asked: how long is the diagonal? By Pythagoras' theorem it is √2. Try as they might, nobody could write √2 as p/q. Legend says the discovery that it is **impossible** was so disturbing that it was kept secret. (The story is a legend; historians are unsure of the details.)

In Class 9 you met **real numbers**: all rational numbers (p/q, q ≠ 0) together with irrational numbers (like √2, √3, π) that cannot be written as p/q. In this chapter you will learn **one powerful theorem** about positive integers — the **Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic** — and use it to:

1. find the **HCF** and **LCM** of numbers by prime factorisation, and see why **HCF × LCM = product** of two numbers;
2. decide whether numbers like 6ⁿ can end in a particular digit;
3. **prove** that √2, √3, √5 and numbers like 3 + 2√5 are irrational.

Think of a positive integer as a building made of **prime bricks**. The theorem says every building has exactly one list of bricks. Once you know a number's bricks, you know almost everything about it.
